    "faq_sunny": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_dressing": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where do I get the Sunny Ginger Ponzu?",
            "text": "<p>Sunny Fine Foods stock it at <a href=\"https://www.sunnyfoods.us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sunnyfoods.us</a>, and it turns up at Whole Foods and Sprouts. If you cannot find it, whisk 3 Tbsp soy sauce, the juice of one lemon and one lime, 1 Tbsp rice vinegar, 1 Tbsp grated fresh ginger and 1 Tbsp neutral oil. It will not be identical &mdash; theirs is never heat pasteurized, which is why the ginger tastes raw &mdash; but it works.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_smash": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why smash the cucumbers instead of slicing them?",
            "text": "<p>Smashing tears the flesh open along its own grain, and those ragged surfaces hold dressing that would slide straight off a clean cut. Press down with the flat of a knife until the cucumber splits, then break it into bite-sized pieces. It is also faster than slicing.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the shrimp are done?",
            "text": "<p><strong>One to two minutes a side</strong> in a properly hot pan. They go from grey and translucent to bright orange and opaque, and the edges pick up a little colour. The reliable visual cue is shape: a shrimp curled into a loose <strong>C</strong> is cooked, one curled into a tight <strong>O</strong> has gone too far.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make it ahead?",
            "text": "<p>Not assembled &mdash; the avocado browns and the cucumber weeps, and the whole appeal is warm shrimp against cold, crunchy vegetables. You can cook the shrimp up to a day ahead and serve them cold, and you can smash the cucumbers a few hours early. Cut the peach and avocado and dress it only when you are ready to eat.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_swap": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My peaches are not ripe. What else works?",
            "text": "<p>Nectarines are the closest swap and are often firmer at the store anyway. Mango works out of season, but peel it, cut it smaller and add extra lime because it runs much sweeter. See the comparison table above. Underripe stone fruit is the one substitution that will genuinely flatten this salad &mdash; if the fruit is hard, use mango instead.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia shrimp?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator, still in the bag, is best. In a hurry, drop the sealed bag in a bowl of cold water for 20&ndash;30 minutes. Then pat them properly dry &mdash; wet shrimp steam instead of searing, and you will never get the crisp edges. Never thaw at room temperature or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_dressing",
        "q_smash",
        "q_done",
        "q_ahead",
        "q_swap",
        "q_defrost"
      ],
